  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A deepening trough will result in a \nstrengthening coastal low providing a shot of rain on Thursday \nwith the potential for a transition to snow in the northeastern \nzones Thursday night.\n\nThe main game in town across central Pennsylvania over the next\nweek will be the potential for the first accumulating snowfall \nmainly for the higher elevations. As a low pressure system \napproaches the region from the Ohio Valley, precipitation \nchances will begin to ramp up tonight with rain likely breaking \nout in an area of 850mb fgen forcing over north central PA. That\narea of fgen forcing will slowly pivot across the northern tier\nand finally translate eastward later Thursday as the upper \nlevel support for the system tracks from west to east across PA.\nThe developing sfc low is progged to be sub 1000 mb as it moves\nacross the Balt-Wash area midday Thursday. The low will \nincrease SE flow and moisture transport from the Mid Atlantic \nCoast / Chesapeake Bay up the Susquehanna Valley. Orographic \neffects will likely lead to an enhancement in rainfall totals \nfrom the highlands of Schuylkill County northward towards \nSullivan County and the Endless Mountains during the daytime\nhours Thursday. This area has the greatest chance of seeing \nover 1.00 inch of rain with this system (prob rain > 1.00 inch\nis 30-40 pct). For much of the rest of central PA, rainfall \namounts are projected to average around a 0.25 inch, with higher\namounts closer to 0.50-0.75 inch possible in the northern tier \nnear the pivot point of the persistent fgen banding. \n\nCold air will wrap in on the back side of the storm. While the\nmain area of precip will have already pulled away from much of\ncentral PA, fgen forcing may support a more persistent comma \nhead lingering across northeast zones, in particular Sullivan \nand back into Potter/Tioga Counties, where steady rain could \ntransition to snow around or shortly after sunset Thursday \nevening. The main questions yet to be ironed out are how quickly\ntemps fall Thursday night, and how long the stratiform precip \nlingers over those zones, but that area will need to be \nmonitored for a potential coating-2\" of snow in the Thursday \nnight - early Friday time frame if the colder/snowier solution \npans out.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Northwesterly flow brings much cooler \ntemperatures this weekend, with lake-enhanced snow showers \ncontinuing Friday into Saturday night.\n\nCooler temperatures and breezy northwesterly winds will take \nhold of the region as the area of low pressure shifts east \ntowards the New England Coast. Wind gusts in the 25-35 mph range\nare likely from Thursday night through the weekend. By sunrise \non Friday, the stratiform precip will be pulling away and north-\nsouth oriented lake effect streamer bands will likely be in its\nwake. Typically streamer bands will trend towards a more \ncellular mode with daytime heating. With highs on Friday in the \n30s and 40s, the dominant ptype will likely be snow up in the \nnorth and west, but rain would be more likely towards the \nsoutheast and where precip rates are lighter. Even the southeast\ncould get some snowflakes if the rates are high enough. The \nlake effect and upslope snow on the back side of this system is \nexpected to produce a coating to locally up to 2 inches of snow \nin the higher elevations of the Laurels and across the northwest\nmountains. \n\nChances for precipitation begin to decrease Saturday night into \nSunday as a subsidence inversion builds in with approaching high\npressure. A slight warming trend emerges into the beginning of \nnext week, with temperatures trending closer to climatological \naverages by Tuesday.",
